'''Renaissance Technologies LLC''' (also known as '''RenTec'''<ref>{{Cite web |date=23 November 2019 |title=Inside Renaissance Technologies Medallion Fund |url=https://atticcapital.com/inside-renaissance-technologies-medallion-fund/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200407025055/https://atticcapital.com/inside-renaissance-technologies-medallion-fund/ |archive-date=7 April 2020 |access-date=7 April 2020 |website=Attic Capital |language=en-US}}</ref> or '''RenTech''')<ref>{{Cite web |last=Weiss |first=Miles |date=1 February 2019 |title=Hedge Fund RenTech Created the Ultimate, Tax-Free IRA Account for Employees |url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2019-02-01/rentech-created-the-ultimate-tax-free-ira-account-for-employees |url-status=live |access-date=7 April 2020 |website=Bloomberg |archive-date=7 April 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200407025052/https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2019-02-01/rentech-created-the-ultimate-tax-free-ira-account-for-employees }}</ref> is an American [[hedge fund]] based in [[East Setauket, New York]],<ref name="bloomberg_July_2014">{{Cite web |last1=Mider |first1=Zachary R. |last2=Rubin |first2=Richard |date=11 July 2014 |title=Renaissance Said Probed by Senate Panel on Tax Maneuver |url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2014-07-12/renaissance-said-probed-by-senate-panel-on-tax-maneuver |access-date=26 December 2015 |publisher=Bloomberg |archive-date=5 January 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160105060315/http://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2014-07-12/renaissance-said-probed-by-senate-panel-on-tax-maneuver |url-status=live }}</ref> on [[Long Island]], that specializes in [[systematic trading]] using [[Quantitative analysis (finance)|quantitative models]] derived from mathematical and [[statistical analysis]]. Renaissance originated as '''Monemetrics''', founded in 1978 by [[mathematician]] and former Cold War [[codebreaker]] [[Jim Simons]], and adopted its current name in 1982.

In 1988, the firm established the Medallion Fund, a form of [[Leonard E. Baum|Leonard Baum]]'s [[Baum–Welch algorithm|mathematical models]] expanded by algebraist [[James Ax]], to explore correlations from which it could profit. The hedge fund was named Medallion in honor of the math awards Simons and Ax had won.<ref name=bloomberg_271107/><ref name="AMS_2014">{{Cite web |title=The American Mathematical Society and the Mathematical Sciences Research Institute's 2014 AMS Einstein Public Lecture in Mathematics with James H. Simons |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Tj1NyJHLvWA |access-date=11 November 2015 |website=youtube.com | date=30 October 2014 |publisher=San Francisco State University |archive-date=16 March 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160316101720/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Tj1NyJHLvWA |url-status=live }}</ref>

Simons ran Renaissance until his retirement in late 2009,<ref name="nytimes.com">{{Cite news |last=PÉREZ-PEÑA |first=RICHARD |date=13 December 2011 |title=Stony Brook University to Get $150 Million Gift |work=The New York Times |agency=New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/14/nyregion/stony-brook-university-given-150-million.html |access-date=28 February 2017 |archive-date=9 September 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170909174341/http://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/14/nyregion/stony-brook-university-given-150-million.html |url-status=live}}</ref> when he was succeeded by [[Peter Fitzhugh Brown|Peter Brown]] and  [[Robert Mercer (businessman)|Robert Mercer]].  
He continued to play a role at the firm as non-executive chairman until 2021. He remained invested in its funds, particularly the Medallion fund, until his death in 2024.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Forbes 400 |url=https://www.forbes.com/profile/james-simons/ |access-date=1 November 2015 |website=Forbes.com |archive-date=31 August 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190831224516/https://www.forbes.com/profile/james-simons/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The company is now run by Brown (after Mercer resigned). Both were [[Computer science|computer scientists]] specializing in [[computational linguistics]] who joined Renaissance in 1993 from [[IBM Research]].<ref name="wsj-160310" /><ref name="reuters-151015">{{Cite news |last=Herbst-Bayliss |first=Svea |date=15 October 2015 |title=Renaissance Technologies to shut small hedge fund: sources |work=Reuters |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-hedgefunds-renaissance-idUSKCN0S72SG20151013 |access-date=31 October 2015 |archive-date=25 October 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151025221000/http://www.reuters.com/article/2015/10/13/us-hedgefunds-renaissance-idUSKCN0S72SG20151013 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="motherj-2014">{{Cite web |last=Blake |first=Mariah |title=Why Are These Hedge Fund Kingpins Dumping Millions Into the Midterms? |url=https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2014/10/hedge-fund-taxes-superpacs-election-renaissance |access-date=14 October 2014 |website=Mother Jones |archive-date=15 October 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141015004129/http://www.motherjones.com/politics/2014/10/hedge-fund-taxes-superpacs-election-renaissance |url-status=live }}</ref> The fund has $165 billion in discretionary assets under management (including leverage) as of April 2021.<ref>{{Cite web|title=The Medallion Fund Is Still Outperforming. Other Renaissance Funds Still Aren't.|url=https://www.institutionalinvestor.com/article/b1rgwjlwfqccst/The-Medallion-Fund-Is-Still-Outperforming-Other-Renaissance-Funds-Still-Aren-t|access-date=19 April 2021|website=Institutional Investor|date=19 April 2021 |language=en-gb}}</ref>

