[[Circumcision]] likely has ancient roots among several ethnic groups in sub-equatorial Africa, Egypt, and Arabia, though the specific form and extent of circumcision has varied. Ritual male circumcision is known to have been practiced by South Sea Islanders, Aboriginal peoples of Australia{{which|date=September 2025}}, Sumatrans, and some ancient Egyptians.<ref>{{Cite journal|last=Cohen|first=Jonathan|date=2011-06-01|title=Male circumcision in the United States: The History, an analysis of the discourse, and a philosophical interpretation|url=https://via.library.depaul.edu/etd/87|journal=College of Liberal Arts & Social Sciences Theses and Dissertations}}</ref>

Today it is still practiced by [[Jews]], [[Samaritans]], [[Druze]],<ref>{{cite book |last=Ubayd |first=Anis |title=The Druze and Their Faith in Tawhid |publisher=Syracuse University Press |year=2006 |isbn=9780815630975 |page=150 |quote=Male circumcision is standard practice, by tradition, among the Druze}}</ref><ref>{{cite book |last=Jacobs |first=Daniel |title=Israel and the Palestinian Territories: The Rough Guide |publisher=Rough Guides |year=1998 |isbn=9781858282480 |page=147 |quote=Circumcision is not compulsory and has no religious significance.}}</ref> [[Coptic Christian]]s,{{efn|name=Christian}}<ref name="Bolnick_2012_ch26">{{cite book |author1-last=Adams |author1-first=Gregory |author2-last=Adams |author2-first=Kristina |year=2012 |chapter=Circumcision in the Early Christian Church: The Controversy That Shaped a Continent |chapter-url=https://books.google.com/books?id=w7zyKB9mdMwC&pg=PA290 |editor1-last=Bolnick |editor1-first=David A. |editor2-last=Koyle |editor2-first=Martin |editor3-last=Yosha |editor3-first=Assaf |title=Surgical Guide to Circumcision |location=[[London]] |publisher=[[Springer-Verlag]] |pages=291–298 |doi=10.1007/978-1-4471-2858-8_26 |isbn=978-1-4471-2857-1}}</ref><ref name="S. Ellwood 2008 95">{{Cite book|last=S. Ellwood|first=Robert|title=The Encyclopedia of World Religions|publisher=[[Infobase Publishing]]|year=2008|isbn=9781438110387|pages=95|quote=It is obligatory among Jews, Muslims, and Coptic Christians. Catholic, Orthodox, and Protestant Christians do not require circumcision. Starting in the last half of the 19th century, however, circumcision also became common among Christians in Europe and especially in North America.}}</ref> [[Ethiopian Orthodox]],{{efn|name=Christian}}<ref name="WHO_2007_GTDPSA">{{cite web |title=Male circumcision: Global trends and determinants of prevalence, safety and acceptability |year=2007 |publisher=World Health Organization |url=http://www.unaids.org/sites/default/files/media_asset/jc1360_male_circumcision_en_0.pdf }}</ref> [[Eritrean Orthodox]],{{efn|name=Christian}}<ref>{{Cite book|last=DeMello|first=Margo|title=Encyclopedia of Body Adornment|publisher=[[ABC-Clio]]|year=2007|isbn=9780313336959|pages=66|quote=Coptic Christians, Ethiopian Orthodox, and Eritrean Orthodox churches on the other hand, do observe the ordainment, and circumcise their sons anywhere from the first week of life to the first few years.}}</ref> [[Muslims]], and some tribes in East and Southern Africa.  Other countries with significant rates of circumcision include the [[United States]], [[South Korea]], and [[Philippines|the Philippines]].<ref>{{cite journal|vauthors=Marck J|year=1997|title=Aspects of male circumcision in sub-equatorial African culture history|journal=Health Transition Review|volume=7 Suppl|issue=supplement|pages=337–60|pmid=10173099}}</ref><ref name="R. Peteet 2017 97–101">{{cite book|title=Spirituality and Religion Within the Culture of Medicine: From Evidence to Practice|first=John|last=R. Peteet|year= 2017| isbn=9780190272432| pages =97–101 |publisher=Oxford University Press|quote=male circumcision is still observed among Ethiopian and Coptic Christians, and circumcision rates are also high today in the Philippines and the US.}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal| title=Estimation of country-specific and global prevalence of male circumcision| journal=National Center for Biotechnology Information| date=2016| pmc=4772313| last1=Morris| first1=B. J.| last2=Wamai| first2=R. G.| last3=Henebeng| first3=E. B.| last4=Tobian| first4=A. A.| last5=Klausner| first5=J. D.| last6=Banerjee| first6=J.| last7=Hankins| first7=C. A.| volume=14| article-number=4| doi=10.1186/s12963-016-0073-5| pmid=26933388| doi-access=free}}</ref>

As practiced in [[ancient Egypt]] and elsewhere in Africa, only part of the foreskin was removed. However, in Judaism and in the United States, the [[foreskin]] is often completely removed. Circumcision and/or [[Penile subincision|subincision]], often as part of an intricate [[coming of age]] ritual, was a common practice among the Aboriginal peoples of Australia{{which|date=September 2025}} and most Pacific islanders{{which|date=September 2025}} at first contact with Western travellers. It is still practiced in the traditional way by some of the population.<ref>{{cite journal | vauthors = Morrison J | title = The origins of the practices of circumcision and subincision among the Aboriginal peoples of Australia | journal = The Medical Journal of Australia | volume = 1 | issue = 3 | pages = 125–7 | date = January 1967 | pmid = 6018441 | doi = 10.5694/j.1326-5377.1967.tb21064.x | s2cid = 45886476 }}</ref><ref name="pmid12737414">{{cite journal | vauthors = Afsari M, Beasley SW, Maoate K, Heckert K | title = Attitudes of Pacific parents to circumcision of boys | journal = Pacific Health Dialog | volume = 9 | issue = 1 | pages = 29–33 | date = March 2002 | pmid = 12737414 | doi = }}</ref>

[[Herodotus]], writing in the 5th century BCE, lists first of all the [[Egyptians]] being the oldest people practicing circumcision then [[Colchis|Colchians]], [[Ethiopia]]ns, [[Phoenicians]], and [[Syria]]ns as circumcising cultures.

